<ReferencePage title="Terraform policy" description="Module boundaries, deployment choices and safe Terraform remediation.">
  <h1>Terraform policy</h1>
  <p>
    Prefer clear names, small typed interfaces, cohesive modules and canonical <code>terraform fmt</code>.
    Source semantics belong in Code Standards; tracked filenames, placement and component contracts
    belong in Repo Standards. Browse the <a href={siteHref('/rules/iac/')}>executable IaC rules</a> for
    exact scope, severity, examples and refusal conditions.
  </p>
  <h2>Comments and safeguards</h2>
  <p>
    SARJ216 bans actual HCL comments, including trailing and expression-embedded comments. Its
    existing policy also covers standalone rationale; those differ from the requested inline ban.
    Strings, URLs, heredoc payloads and embedded shell or YAML are data. Terraform JSON's block-body
    <code>"//"</code> property is not an HCL comment. SARJ202 and SARJ207 retain the narrower banner,
    commented-code and restatement controls. Use names, concise descriptions and module documentation
    for explanation. Required legal notices, generated provenance and functional tool directives need
    a narrow reviewed file exception with an owner and reason; never remove a directive to silence a rule.
  </p>
  <p>
    SARJ222 prohibits every variable validation block as organization policy, even when its condition
    is useful. This removes a convenient Terraform-native input safeguard: detection does not prove
    deletion safe. Identify the invariant, authoritative owner and equivalent enforcement before a
    reviewed migration. Existing immutable safeguard contracts remain enforced and emit a migration
    warning for retained variable validations; uncontracted or changed guards block. Do not create
    source snapshots as a workaround. SARJ209 removes only assertions proved redundant under the
    exact type and nullability semantics, and refuses protected contracts and override modules. Terraform validation,
    provider checks and lifecycle safeguards are distinct controls; this is not a request to delete them.
  </p>
  <h2>Deployment choices and module interfaces</h2>
  <p>
    SARJ219 inspects structured deployment contexts and templates, with complete sibling-module facts.
    Reuse an existing resource or discovery attribute before adding an input. Explicit composition
    roots and tfvars may supply concrete deployment choices; backend restrictions, provider constants,
    protocol constants, module sources, examples and schema data have separate valid boundaries.
    A reusable module's configurable default must be intentional and documented. A constant local
    does not make a deployment assumption reusable. No literal-to-binding autofix has established
    equivalence for all supported inputs.
  </p>
  <p>
    TFLint 0.65.0's Terraform ruleset 0.15.0 finds unused inputs across files. Sarj runs each module
    directory, including JSON-only modules, as a root and normalizes its upstream warning to an error.
    A tfvars assignment is not use. Removing the declaration can still break callers or published
    contracts, so broad TFLint deletion fixes are disabled. SARJ223 reports unused child outputs only
    for explicitly reviewed <code>iac.no-unused-module-outputs.internal_modules</code> boundaries,
    retaining existing external-output contracts. Indexes, splats and whole-module consumers count;
    root, public, remote-state, script and unavailable external usage remain unknown unless trustworthy
    contract evidence closes the boundary. Unknown does not mean unused or proved live.
  </p>
  <h2>A finite anti-pattern inventory</h2>
  <p>
    Maintained upstream controls cover deprecated interpolation, index and two-argument lookup syntax,
    empty-list equality, duplicate map keys, unpinned module sources, missing provider/version
    declarations, untyped variables and unused declarations. Sarj adds environment dispatch and dead
    inputs (SARJ204–205), redundant assertions and condition resources (SARJ209–210), nested and complex
    conditionals (SARJ215, SARJ217), repeated deployment literals (SARJ218), and conditional presence
    review (SARJ221). This finite inventory cannot establish every design flaw. The presence of
    <code>count</code>, <code>for_each</code>, <code>depends_on</code>, <code>lifecycle</code>,
    <code>dynamic</code>, <code>try</code> or <code>can</code> alone is not a violation.
  </p>
  <h2>Layout and retirement</h2>
  <p>
    Keep each module's declarations in its own <code>variables.tf</code> and <code>outputs.tf</code>
    (or JSON counterparts), without empty files or trivial modules. Terraform does not recursively
    load declaration-kind subfolders. SARJ224 checks blocks; Repo Standards checks detached folders
    and advises review of meaningful filename clusters. Names do not prove cohesion or justify
    extraction. Preserve flat composition, tfvars loading, provider mappings, backends and entrypoints.
    New layout rules start as warnings; blocking requires a separate reviewed lifecycle promotion.
  </p>
  <p>
    SARJ220 reuses <code>Retire by:</code>, <code>Remove when:</code> and <code>Permanent:</code>
    description metadata. Dates, defaults and observed environment values cannot prove rollout
    readiness. Review supported configurations, rollback and count/index/key addresses before retiring
    a flag. SARJ221's recommendations retain that distinction.
  </p>
  <h2>Automatic changes and review boundaries</h2>
  <p>
    Supported Core <code>check --fix</code>, <code>fix</code> and staged checks use the real fix pathway.
    Safe profiles cover existing lexical cruft fixes, empty ordinary comments, precisely redundant
    unprotected assertions, closed homogeneous literal selections, and literal numeric count folding
    that retains count and instance addresses. Nonempty rationale, legal notices and directives require
    review. Symbolic gates, input/output deletion, hardcoded binding replacement, JSON declaration
    movement and module extraction have no established safe automatic transformation.
  </p>
  <p>
    SARJ224 can consolidate native HCL declarations into existing selected files in the same module.
    It refuses comments, guards, overrides, duplicate interfaces, source-reading functions, missing
    destinations and incomplete staged context. The planner compares complete module structures;
    Core checks source bytes, module membership and the index, applies a rollback transaction and
    requires review before staging. Provider-free fixture plans verify combined fixes preserve planned
    values and instance addresses; a second pass must be clean. Address-changing refactors require
    reviewed migration and plan evidence, beyond a generated moved block or syntax comparison.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both <code>.tf</code> and <code>.tf.json</code> are supported deliberately. Tfvars are input values,
    not declaration sources. Generated, vendor, examples and fixture scope is rule-specific and stated
    in each contract. Malformed, oversized or unsupported module sources fail qualification rather
    than proving a clean result. See HashiCorp's
    <a href="https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/syntax/configuration">syntax</a>,
    <a href="https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/syntax/json">JSON syntax</a>,
    <a href="https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/modules/develop/composition">module composition</a>,
    <a href="https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/modules/develop/structure">module structure</a>,
    <a href="https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/modules/develop/refactoring">refactoring</a>
    and <a href="https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/tests">testing</a> documentation.
  </p>
</ReferencePage>
